  {
    "episode_id": 74275,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74275.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 4,
    "turn_text": "Let me just say a few quick words of introduction and thanks and then we'll start the conversation. Congressman Coles in his 10th term in the House of Representatives representing the fourth district in Oklahoma. He currently serves. as the vice-ranking member of the full appropriations committee, and he's ranking member of the subcommittee of labor, health and human services, education and related agencies, terribly important for all of these global health security matters we're talking about. He's also a senior member of the House Appropriations Subcommittee on Defense, which obviously also has a very important role when we're talking about health security at home and abroad. He's the ranking member of the Full House Rules Committee, where he served since 2013, and is a deputy whip of the Republican Conference. He's also a member and a role member of the Chickasaw Nation, one of six members serving in Congress who are Native Americans. Congressman Cole has kindly served For the past three years on the CSIS Commission on strengthening America's health security, it's been terribly important and valuable for Tom Congressman for to have you be with us in that commission will be continuing through 2022. Very, very grateful to you for that. Also very grateful your staff, Sabrina Parker, Shane Hand, and Josh Brogus have been terribly helpful to us. Here on the CSIS side, Ameth Mondavili, John Montz is our producer today, and very grateful to them. So let's start our conversation. Last week was the 100th anniversary. of the terrible massacre of the Black Wall Street neighborhood, the Greenwood community in Tulsa, Oklahoma. You were active in that period, congressionally in memorializing that, but also I just wanted to ask you for your reflections on that moment. It was a big historical moment of reflection.",
