  {
    "episode_id": 74275,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74275.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, it's early days. We haven't come around 100% on this because we still have questions. But I'm a big fan of DARPA, which I think has done great work. It's high risk, high reward kind of investment. But no question that the reward has more than merited the investment in the defense area. So the concept is an appealing concept. I think our big concern initially, and I've had extensive private discussions with Dr. Collins about this, other people in NIH. Number one, we didn't want something new that was separate from. If this is a new part of NIH, a new mechanism within it, that's good. We didn't need competing agency, so to speak, out here. That looks like that's the plan. I think NIH initially had the same concerns. I've been around particularly on this committee on education, I'll use an example, where people tend to get attracted to whatever the shiny new object is. I remember a number of years ago when President Obama was there and the education program was something called Race to the Top. Well, Race to the Top sounded wonderful and it was great, but it was funded at the expense of programs like TRIO and gear up that have a longer history and that are very successful, producing millions of college graduates for the United States from populations that, I mean, most of these kids are first generation college students. So, you know, we were cannibalizing proven programs to try and do something new that wasn't likely to last past an administration and then race to the top no longer around, so to speak. So I think you fund the basic program. We want to make sure that Harper fits that model, that it lets us utilize the institutes across the NIH in a creative way, form partnerships with private entities. We certainly saw that in COVID-19. I remember Dr. Collins and we had multiple discussions over the course of this pandemic telling me, he said, Tom, we're doing things in weeks and months that we haven't done would have taken years beforehand. So we really saw some, and if that's what this is, then I'm for it. That's second concern, and I made a point in the hearing when I raised this with Secretary Becerra. I said, I want you to understand this does not change my commitment to your top line number. $6.5 billion is a lot of money. Now it's not a one-shot deal, it's over a three-year period. But as I recall the DARPA, you know budgets around 3.8 billion something like that if I remember from our hearings and you know is that's just make sure we don't over fund this at the beginning I would want to keep the top line budget that the administration is proposing and and if this is the right number grade if it's if we should start a little bit lower as we sort of build the protocols and make sure this works and it pans out I don't want something that that doesn't work, although I'm, again, I walk into it with the expectation that it would and the hope that it would. But $6.5 billion is a big bet. And I might want to start at the table with a little bit smaller bet and put that money. I'll give you an example. We're only funding. 11, 12% of the proposals we're getting at the National Cancer Institute right now. And it's not because we're not doing more than we've ever done. I mean, we are. But over the course of the entire NIH, we're probably in funding 20, 22, 23% of the proposals. Our problem is the scientific breakthroughs in cancer right now are just coming at extraordinary speed. This is a real moment of cancer research. And so I want to make sure we're funding enough there. And again, we've made progress over the last few years. We were down as low as I think 8% of funding of cancer And that's still our number one killer. That's over 600,000 Americans a year. I have cancer and that's where the recovery rate of over 60% that we have now is an extraordinary achievement. But we're losing as many people to cancer every year as we've lost in the pandemic over the last year or magnitude. So those are the type of questions. But again, I want to I don't want to be critical here. I want to laud the Biden administration for making a substantial commitment and a substantial and important proposal and introducing a new idea. Now it's our job as legislators to ask tough questions and to make sure we use the resources, but we're on the same page. And in this area, you know, I really look forward to working with an administration that I know is committed to making substantial investments in biomedical research and public health preparedness.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
